        ],
        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "example_question",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Simplify <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\sqrt{x} \\cdot \\sqrt{x^3}.]</span> </p>",
            "readable_text": "Simplify [MATH: \\sqrt{x} \\cdot \\sqrt{x^3}.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>EXPLANATION</p>",
            "readable_text": "EXPLANATION",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "example_explanation",
            "normalized_html": "<p>We perform the multiplication under a single radical and then simplify the result:</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\eqalign{\n\\sqrt{x} \\cdot \\sqrt{x^3} &amp;= \\\n\\sqrt{x \\cdot x^3} &amp;= \\\n\\sqrt{x^4} &amp;= \\\n|x^2|\n}]</span></p><p>A squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: |x^{2}||= x^{2}.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "We perform the multiplication under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\n\\sqrt{x} \\cdot \\sqrt{x^3} &= \\\n\\sqrt{x \\cdot x^3} &= \\\n\\sqrt{x^4} &= \\\n|x^2|\n}]\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n[MATH: |x^{2}||= x^{2}.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Example Prompt:\nSimplify [MATH: \\sqrt{x} \\cdot \\sqrt{x^3}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe perform the multiplication under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\n\\sqrt{x} \\cdot \\sqrt{x^3} &= \\\n\\sqrt{x \\cdot x^3} &= \\\n\\sqrt{x^4} &= \\\n|x^2|\n}]\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n[MATH: |x^{2}||= x^{2}.]"
      },
