    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nThe product rule for radicals states that the square root of a product equals the product of the square roots:\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\n\\sqrt{a} \\cdot \\sqrt{b} = \\sqrt{ab}\n}]\n\nContent:\nWe can use the product rule to combine radical expressions.\n\nContent:\nFor example, let's suppose we want to combine the radicals in the following expression:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\sqrt{5x} \\cdot \\sqrt{6z}]\n\nContent:\nTo combine the radicals, we apply the product rule as follows:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: sqrt(5x) \u22c5 sqrt(6z)|=; sqrt(5x \u22c5 6z)|=; sqrt(30xz)]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Combining Radical Expressions\n\nExample Prompt:\nFind an expression that's equal to [MATH: \\sqrt{5w^3} \\cdot \\sqrt{uv}] by combining the radicals.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe apply the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n[MATH: sqrt(5w^{3}) \u22c5 sqrt(uv)|=; sqrt(5w^{3} \u22c5 uv)|=; sqrt(5uvw^{3})]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: sqrt(5x^{3}) \u22c5 sqrt(2y) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: sqrt(10x^{3}y)]\nb. [MATH: sqrt(5x^{3}y)]\nc. [MATH: sqrt(x^{3}y)]\nd. [MATH: sqrt(10xy)]\ne. [MATH: sqrt(10y)]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nThe expression [MATH: sqrt(3x) \u22c5 sqrt(7y)] is equivalent to\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: sqrt(xy)]\nb. [MATH: sqrt(21x^{2}y^{2})]\nc. [MATH: sqrt(7xy)]\nd. [MATH: sqrt(21)]\ne. [MATH: sqrt(21xy)]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Combining Radical Expressions Using the Rules of Absolute Value\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify [MATH: \\sqrt{x} \\cdot \\sqrt{x^3}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe perform the multiplication under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\n\\sqrt{x} \\cdot \\sqrt{x^3} &= \\\n\\sqrt{x \\cdot x^3} &= \\\n\\sqrt{x^4} &= \\\n|x^2|\n}]\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n[MATH: |x^{2}||= x^{2}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 2sqrt(5x) \u22c5 sqrt(5x^{3}) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 10x^{2}]\nb. [MATH: 10x^{2}]\nc. [MATH: sqrt(10)x^{2}]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 sqrt(10)x]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 sqrt(10)x^{2}]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify [MATH: sqrt(12x^{2}y^{3}) \u22c5 sqrt(2xy^{4})] given that [MATH: x, y \u2265 0.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 2xy^{3}sqrt(xy)]\nb. [MATH: 2xy^{3}sqrt(6xy)]\nc. [MATH: 4xy^{2}sqrt(6xy^{2})]\nd. [MATH: 2xy^{3}sqrt(6xy^{2})]\ne. [MATH: 2xy^{3}sqrt(x^{2}y)]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Tutorial: The Product Rule for Cube Roots\n\nContent:\nThe product rule for radicals also works for higher-order roots, like the cube root. In the case of the cube root, we have\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\sqrt[3]{ab} = \\sqrt[3]{a}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{b}.]\n\nContent:\nIn general,\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\sqrt[n]{ab} = \\sqrt[n]{a}\\cdot \\sqrt[n]{b}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Example: Simplifying a Cube Root Expression\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify [MATH: \\sqrt[3]{16x^2}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{4x}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe perform the multiplication under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n[MATH: root(16x^{2}, 3) \u22c5 root(4x, 3)|=; root(16x^{2} \u22c5 4x, 3)|=; root(64x^{3}, 3)|=; root((4x)^{3}, 3)|=; 4x]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: root(2x, 3) \u22c5 root(3y, 3) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: root(5xy, 3)]\nb. [MATH: root(xy^{3}, 3)]\nc. [MATH: root(6xy, 3)]\nd. [MATH: root(6xy, 6)]\ne. [MATH: root(5xy, 6)]\n\n---\n\nItem 11 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 2root(2x, 3) \u22c5 root(4x^{2}, 3) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 4x]\nb. [MATH: 8x^{3}]\nc. [MATH: 8x]\nd. [MATH: 2root(2, 3)x]\ne. [MATH: 2root(2, 3)x^{2}]"
